  <subsection xml:id="subsec-reading-a-graph">
    <title>Reading a Graph</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-reading-a-graph">
      <title>Reading a Graph</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Use the graph below to answer the questions.</p>
        
        <image source="images/vocabulary-graph.png" width="50%">
          <shortdescription>Graph with vertices A through E and six undirected edges.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              An undirected graph with vertices A, B, C, D, and E. The edges are AB, AC,
              BC, CD, CE, and DE.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>How many vertices and edges are present?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Find the degree of each vertex.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Give one path from A to E.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Give one circuit that starts and ends at C.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Is the graph connected? Explain.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Are there any loops? If not add one.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <exercises>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-crossing-without-vertex">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two edges cross in a drawing, but no dot appears at the crossing. Is the crossing a
          vertex? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-equivalent-graph-drawings">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Can two different-looking drawings represent the same graph? What must remain
          unchanged?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-connected-degree-zero">
      <statement>
        <p>Can a connected graph contain a vertex of degree zero? Explain.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>